data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (ships with .NET 11), the **sync-over-async bridge for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The opt-in you used in .NET 10 (`EnableSyncOverAsync` / warning suppression) no longer exists.

All synchronous LINQ operators and `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos provider will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

### Affected calls in your code:

| Sync (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Migration

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Why the removal?

The Cosmos DB SDK is async-only. The sync bridge used `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` internally, which risks deadlocks and thread-pool starvation. EF Core 10 deprecated it with a warning; EF Core 11 removes it completely.

**Bottom line:** Every call site touching your repository must go `async`/`await` up the chain. There is no workaround short of switching to a relational provider.
